Yes — we can build a visualizer for both 0/1 Knapsack and TSP, but I recommend developing TSP first, as you suggested, and adding Knapsack once the core visualization works.
Your main goal isn't simply to animate Branch and Bound. It is to show the algorithmic reasoning behind every decision: how a bound is calculated, why a node is selected, why a branch is pruned, and how the best solution changes.
For TSP, that reasoning can be especially visual because we can display a cost matrix, a search tree, partial tours, lower-bound calculations, and the best complete tour side by side.
1. What our TSP visualizer should show
A. City graph
Display cities as nodes and distances as weighted edges. Highlight the current partial tour and the edge being considered.
B. Cost matrix and lower bound
Show the original distance matrix, the reduced matrix, each row/column reduction, and the resulting lower bound with the arithmetic explained.
C. Search tree
Display every generated node, its partial path, current cost, lower bound, status, and reason for exploring or pruning it.
These are the three core views of our own interface, not a requirement to copy any existing tool's design.
2. How each algorithmic step will work
We'll use a matrix-reduction Branch and Bound approach for TSP. This gives us numerical calculations we can explain at every step.
Step 1 — Initialize the problem
Take the cities and distances, choose a starting city, and create the distance matrix. Missing edges and forbidden moves are represented as infinity.
Step 2 — Calculate the root lower bound
Reduce each matrix row by its smallest finite value, then each column by its smallest finite value. Add all reductions to obtain the root's lower bound.
Step 3 — Branch
Select a city transition and create child nodes representing alternative next-city choices. Each child represents a partial tour.
Step 4 — Calculate each child's bound
For the selected edge, use its reduced-matrix cost, forbid the row and column that would violate the chosen transition, prevent premature subtours, and reduce the remaining matrix. Add the new reduction cost to the parent's bound and selected-edge cost.
Step 5 — Select the next node
Use the best-bound strategy: expand the eligible node with the smallest lower bound. The visualizer will show why that node is selected.
Step 6 — Prune or continue
For a minimization problem, if a node's valid lower bound is greater than or equal to the best complete tour cost found, that node cannot improve the answer and can be pruned. Invalid subtours are rejected too.
Step 7 — Find and verify the optimum
When a complete valid tour is found, update the best-known tour if its cost is lower. Finish when no eligible node can improve it.
The key is that every action will show both what happened and why it is mathematically valid.
